# Form Component Rules

Apply these rules whenever building or updating forms in the project.

## Global Select Rule

Use the project’s reusable **searchable select** component for relational or long-option select fields.

**Preferred component:** `SearchableSelect`

Use this component instead of building one-off select/dropdown implementations when the field selects entities or from medium/large option lists.

**Examples:** class selector, section selector, subject selector, teacher selector, student selector, role selector, department selector, designation selector.

## When to Use SearchableSelect

Use `SearchableSelect` by default when:

- the field selects from dynamic data
- the field selects related entities
- the option list may grow
- search would improve usability
- consistent reusable form UX is preferred

## When Plain Select Is Acceptable

A normal non-searchable select is acceptable only when:

- the option list is very small and static
- the field has fewer than about 5 obvious choices
- a native select provides better UX for the case

**Examples:** status (active/inactive), yes/no, gender (small static list), simple enum-like fields.

## Consistency Rule

Do not create new custom searchable dropdown implementations in feature modules. Always reuse the shared searchable select component unless there is a strong technical reason not to. If a new use case needs additional behavior, extend the shared component instead of duplicating logic.

## Styling and Integration

- All searchable selects must follow the project’s established form styling and admin design system. Do not introduce visually inconsistent select widgets.
- Integrate searchable selects with: labels, validation messages, disabled state, loading state, preselected values.

## Reusability Rule

If any feature needs selectable relational data:

- Prefer reusing `SearchableSelect`
- Normalize options for `labelKey` and `valueKey`
- Keep form components consistent across modules
